    "result_text": "All 36 tests pass with 0 failures and 0 errors.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented E.164 national-number length validation in the telephone normalization logic.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `OwnerRestControllerV1.java` (`normalizeTelephone`):\n\nAfter the existing general E.164 format check (`+` followed by 8\u201315 digits), I added a `requireValidNationalLength(digits)` call backed by a small country-code table:\n\n```java\nprivate static final Map<String, Integer> NATIONAL_NUMBER_LENGTHS = Map.of(\"61\", 9, \"1\", 10);\n```\n\n`requireValidNationalLength` matches the country code by **longest prefix first** (so `+61` is matched before the `+1` prefix it happens to start with), computes the national number length as `digits.length() - countryCode.length()`, and throws `InvalidFieldsException([\"telephone\"])` \u2014 which the exception advice maps to **HTTP 400** \u2014 when the length doesn't match. Country codes not in the table (e.g. `+64`) are left to the general length check, so existing behavior is preserved.\n\n**Verification against the tests:**\n- `Cp26` `+61 123` \u2192 national `123` (3 digits) \u2260 9 \u2192 **400** \u2713\n- `Cp08`/`Cp02` `0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678`, national 9 digits \u2713\n- `Cp08` `+64 21 123 456` \u2192 `+6421123456`, no fixed-length rule \u2192 accepted \u2713\n- `Cp03` national/international `+61` forms both normalize to `+61412345678` \u2713\n\nI also added the `java.util.Comparator` import. Full suite: **36 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors.**",
